@host = http://localhost:5080

### What the shop sells. Catalog listed these on first start, and Ordering learned their prices from the
### ProductListedV1 messages a second later: it never asks Catalog anything at checkout.
GET {{host}}/products

### What is on the shelf. Two mugs only, on purpose.
GET {{host}}/stock

### A malformed address is refused, field by field, without anything throwing.
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{ "street": "", "city": "Utrecht", "postalCode": "nope", "lines": [] }

### A SKU nobody sells: 400 from the domain service that prices the lines, before an order exists.
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "street": "Oudegracht 1",
  "city": "Utrecht",
  "postalCode": "3511 AA",
  "lines": [{ "sku": "TEAPOT", "quantity": 1 }]
}

### A good address, a line with no SKU. 422 before an order exists in the database at all: the handler
### builds the aggregate, asks it what that broke, and refuses. The order answered for its line, and
### nothing was added to a context, queried or saved to find that out.
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "street": "Oudegracht 1",
  "city": "Utrecht",
  "postalCode": "3511 AA",
  "lines": [{ "sku": "   ", "quantity": 1 }]
}

### The same SKU twice: 422 again, but the code is CheckInvariants, because that rule stayed a seam.
### Only the order can see both lines, which is why the rule is the root's rather than the line's, and
### it is the same one call that found the line's rule above. The boundary answers for both.
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "street": "Oudegracht 1",
  "city": "Utrecht",
  "postalCode": "3511 AA",
  "lines": [{ "sku": "MUG", "quantity": 1 }, { "sku": "MUG", "quantity": 1 }]
}

### A good one. Note the ORD_ prefix on the id that comes back, and the status: Placed. Inventory and
### Payments have not answered yet.
# @name placed
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "street": "Oudegracht 1",
  "city": "Utrecht",
  "postalCode": "3511 AA",
  "lines": [{ "sku": "COFFEE-1KG", "quantity": 2 }]
}

### A second or two later: stock reserved, payment taken, Confirmed. The id parses with or without its
### prefix.
GET {{host}}/orders/{{placed.response.body.id}}

### Shipping booked a van on the confirmation. It never saw an Order.
GET {{host}}/shipments

### Cancelling a confirmed order: 422, ORDER_ALREADY_CONFIRMED. Cancel did what it was told, the order
### was asked whether the result may exist, and a named rule said no. Nothing was saved.
POST {{host}}/orders/{{placed.response.body.id}}/cancel
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reason": "Changed my mind." }

### More than the payment provider accepts. Inventory reserves the grinder, Payments is refused, Ordering
### cancels, and Inventory puts the grinder back: the compensating half of the process.
# @name declined
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "street": "Oudegracht 1",
  "city": "Utrecht",
  "postalCode": "3511 AA",
  "lines": [{ "sku": "GRINDER", "quantity": 1 }]
}

### Cancelled, with the provider's refusal in Payments' words.
GET {{host}}/orders/{{declined.response.body.id}}

### Three mugs of two: Inventory refuses, and nothing is set aside, not even the two it had.
# @name refused
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "street": "Oudegracht 1",
  "city": "Utrecht",
  "postalCode": "3511 AA",
  "lines": [{ "sku": "MUG", "quantity": 3 }]
}

GET {{host}}/orders/{{refused.response.body.id}}

### Every payment and why: one captured, one declined, one voided because its order was cancelled first.
GET {{host}}/payments

### The grinder is available again, the coffee is reserved.
GET {{host}}/stock

### Repricing bumps the product's Version. Two of these at once gives one caller a 409. Ordering hears
### about the new price a second later; orders already placed keep the price they were placed at.
PUT {{host}}/products/MUG/price
Content-Type: application/json

{ "price": 9.50 }
